According to a latest report by IntelMarketResearch, the global Gene Silencing Market was valued at USD 5 billion in 2025 and is projected to reach USD 10 billion by 2034, expanding at a CAGR of 8.2% during 2025–2034. The market is being driven by advances in RNA interference, antisense oligonucleotides, CRISPR-based technologies, expanding therapeutic pipelines, increasing research into genetic and rare diseases, and improvements in drug-delivery technologies.
Gene silencing encompasses technologies that suppress the expression of specific genes at transcriptional or post-transcriptional stages. Core approaches include RNA interference (RNAi), antisense oligonucleotides (ASOs), and CRISPR/Cas-based systems. These technologies are being investigated and developed across oncology, neurological diseases, rare genetic disorders, and other therapeutic areas.
